course: dictionary
lesson: 3995
title: Gamblers and risky words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A gambler is a person who plays games of chance for money, such as cards, betting or slot machines. The word can also mean someone who takes big risks, hoping to win something. When you meet a word like this, look at the words around it for clues.
explain: «A heavy gambler» suggests someone who gambles a lot, and «a gambler's luck» is the luck a gambler hopes to have. In «The gambler's fallacy is thinking a result is "due" because it hasn't happened for a while», the quotation marks around "due" tell you the idea is being questioned. Fallacy means a false belief, so the sentence is warning you about a mistake.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. Try "someone who plays games of chance for money" in place of gambler: «Thinking a result is "due" because it hasn't come up for a while is called the gambler's fallacy» still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
fact: A gambler is a person who plays games of chance for money, or who takes big risks hoping to win.
fact: «The gambler's fallacy» is thinking a result is "due" because it hasn't happened for a while.
fact: In gambling, the house usually wins, and the chances of losing are high.
fact: Gamblers can win or lose money on cards, betting and slot machines.
fact: Context clues, such as «a heavy gambler» and «a gambler's luck», help you work out a word's meaning.
exercise: «Thinking a result is "due" because it hasn't come up for a while is called the gambler's fallacy.» What is a gambler? | A person who stops for a long rest | A teacher who checks sums | *Someone who plays games of chance for money | A person who writes about due results
exercise: What is the gambler's fallacy? | A kind of slot machine | Losing money on a horse race | *Thinking a result is due because it hasn't happened for a while | Winning every game of cards
exercise: In «a heavy gambler», what does heavy suggest? | A gambler who never plays | A gambler who weighs a lot | A gambler who is very quiet | *A gambler who gambles a lot
test: Ben says the coin must land on heads soon because it hasn't for ages. Which idea is this? | *The gambler's fallacy | A game of chance with no risk | The house always loses | A safe way to save money
test: In gambling, what usually happens? | Nobody ever loses money | The gambler always wins | *The house usually wins and the chances of losing are high | Everyone gets their money back
exam: Which phrase means the luck a gambler hopes to have? | the gambler's fallacy | *a gambler's luck | a safe bet with no risk | a heavy gambler
